{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ws51qfMe3uEw6dHqmYnJFX","type":"tool_result","content":"5:# Chapter 11: Below the Waterline\n39:> *\"The cargo does not belong to the crew. It was aboard before this watch, and it will be aboard after.\"*\n40:> — Lodestar Ledgers\n107:> **Extended Analogy:** Think of the ship's hold, below the waterline.\n109:> The cargo is not part of the crew. The crew is relieved and replaced on a schedule that has nothing to do with what is in the hold. Watches change, hands sign off at the end of a voyage, a new complement comes aboard for the next one. Through all of it, the cargo sits where it was stowed.\n111:> The hold is inventoried, claimed, and released by a process that runs on entirely different rails from the watch rotation. A shipper files a claim against space in the hold. A quartermaster decides which part of the hold satisfies it. When the claim is released, what happens next — is the cargo landed, is it held for the next voyage, is it destroyed — was settled by the terms of the arrangement long before anyone came to collect it.\n188:[Omitted long matching line]\n190:An escape hatch is precisely what it is, and escape hatches open both ways.\n296:                    │  watches for new PVCs, │\n319:[Omitted long matching line]\n406:- **A is wrong**, and it is the reversal to watch for. It has the direction of the relationship backwards: claims find volumes, not the other way around, and the Pod's reference terminates at the claim.\n439:🗺️→🌊→🌅 · **Voyage Progress: you are through the harbor and into open water.**\n569:This is the third sighting of the same light. An Ingress with no Ingress controller routes nothing. A NetworkPolicy on a CNI plugin that doesn't implement policy blocks nothing. A StorageClass with no running provisioner creates nothing. Each time, the object exists, `kubectl get` shows it, `kubectl describe` shows a sensible spec, and nothing happens, because the object was always only ever a request addressed to a component that has to be separately installed and running.\n861:### The fourth sighting\n1095:[Omitted long matching line]\n1337:| **`hostPath`** | A powerful escape hatch with many security risks. Avoid it; prefer a `local` PersistentVolume. Makes Pods silently node-dependent. |\n1355:## The Voyage Ahead\n1371:[Omitted long matching line]\n1373:> *\"The hold is inventoried by people who never sail. That is not a failure of the arrangement. That is the arrangement.\"*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1d9b807e-74b1-4e23-a469-c2ab5f0edd1f","uuid":"23a61d88-878c-4223-b92d-2cb6f1baca94","timestamp":"2026-08-25T07:30:41.979Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"5:# Chapter 11: Below the Waterline\n39:> *\"The cargo does not belong to the crew. It was aboard before this watch, and it will be aboard after.\"*\n40:> — Lodestar Ledgers\n107:> **Extended Analogy:** Think of the ship's hold, below the waterline.\n109:> The cargo is not part of the crew. The crew is relieved and replaced on a schedule that has nothing to do with what is in the hold. Watches change, hands sign off at the end of a voyage, a new complement comes aboard for the next one. Through all of it, the cargo sits where it was stowed.\n111:> The hold is inventoried, claimed, and released by a process that runs on entirely different rails from the watch rotation. A shipper files a claim against space in the hold. A quartermaster decides which part of the hold satisfies it. When the claim is released, what happens next — is the cargo landed, is it held for the next voyage, is it destroyed — was settled by the terms of the arrangement long before anyone came to collect it.\n188:[Omitted long matching line]\n190:An escape hatch is precisely what it is, and escape hatches open both ways.\n296:                    │  watches for new PVCs, │\n319:[Omitted long matching line]\n406:- **A is wrong**, and it is the reversal to watch for. It has the direction of the relationship backwards: claims find volumes, not the other way around, and the Pod's reference terminates at the claim.\n439:🗺️→🌊→🌅 · **Voyage Progress: you are through the harbor and into open water.**\n569:This is the third sighting of the same light. An Ingress with no Ingress controller routes nothing. A NetworkPolicy on a CNI plugin that doesn't implement policy blocks nothing. A StorageClass with no running provisioner creates nothing. Each time, the object exists, `kubectl get` shows it, `kubectl describe` shows a sensible spec, and nothing happens, because the object was always only ever a request addressed to a component that has to be separately installed and running.\n861:### The fourth sighting\n1095:[Omitted long matching line]\n1337:| **`hostPath`** | A powerful escape hatch with many security risks. Avoid it; prefer a `local` PersistentVolume. Makes Pods silently node-dependent. |\n1355:## The Voyage Ahead\n1371:[Omitted long matching line]\n1373:> *\"The hold is inventoried by people who never sail. That is not a failure of the arrangement. That is the arrangement.\"*","numLines":19,"totalLines":19}}
