{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017cEDM2AnAaiWBDXFbMc8bw","type":"tool_result","content":"186\t> 🪝 **Snag:** By default there is *no* cap. The documentation is blunt: *there is no limit on how much space an `emptyDir` or `hostPath` volume can consume, and no isolation between containers or Pods* [source: k8s-docs-volumes-csi-and-subpath-2026-08-25]. A runaway log writer in one Pod can fill the node's disk and put every other Pod on that node into disk pressure. `sizeLimit` exists because the default is unbounded.\n187\t\n188\t**`hostPath`** mounts a file or directory from the host node's filesystem into your Pod [source: k8s-docs-volume-types-depth-2026-08-25]. It has an optional `type` field alongside the required `path`, which controls what checks Kubernetes performs before mounting: whether the path must already exist, must be a directory, may be created, must be a socket, and so on [source: k8s-docs-volume-types-depth-2026-08-25]. The documentation's own framing of `hostPath` is exactly right: *this is not something that most Pods will need, but it offers a powerful escape hatch for some applications* [source: k8s-docs-volume-types-depth-2026-08-25].\n189\t\n190\tAn escape hatch is precisely what it is, and escape hatches open both ways.\n191\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1d9b807e-74b1-4e23-a469-c2ab5f0edd1f","uuid":"d960d5ca-2988-4476-b921-a231adb36948","timestamp":"2026-08-25T07:31:43.003Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-11\\draft-v1.md","content":"> 🪝 **Snag:** By default there is *no* cap. The documentation is blunt: *there is no limit on how much space an `emptyDir` or `hostPath` volume can consume, and no isolation between containers or Pods* [source: k8s-docs-volumes-csi-and-subpath-2026-08-25]. A runaway log writer in one Pod can fill the node's disk and put every other Pod on that node into disk pressure. `sizeLimit` exists because the default is unbounded.\n\n**`hostPath`** mounts a file or directory from the host node's filesystem into your Pod [source: k8s-docs-volume-types-depth-2026-08-25]. It has an optional `type` field alongside the required `path`, which controls what checks Kubernetes performs before mounting: whether the path must already exist, must be a directory, may be created, must be a socket, and so on [source: k8s-docs-volume-types-depth-2026-08-25]. The documentation's own framing of `hostPath` is exactly right: *this is not something that most Pods will need, but it offers a powerful escape hatch for some applications* [source: k8s-docs-volume-types-depth-2026-08-25].\n\nAn escape hatch is precisely what it is, and escape hatches open both ways.\n","numLines":6,"startLine":186,"totalLines":1373}}}
